INT. SALT MINE ELEVATOR CAGE - DEEP SHAFT - NIGHT

The cage hangs motionless in blackness. White crystalline walls sweat. A single caged bulb flickers, turning five faces the color of old teeth.

MAURA KELLEHER, 50s, foreman's helmet, grips the lever. DESMOND "DEZ" OYELOWO, 20s, wide-eyed, presses his back to the bars. TRUDY NAKASHIMA, 30s, geologist, clutches a clipboard. OLD HENRY BASKIN, 70s, silent, stares upward. And little JOEL, 12, the foreman's nephew, crouches on the floor with his hands over his ears.

Somewhere far below, something scrapes. Slow. Patient.

                    MAURA
Everybody stay calm.

                    DEZ
Calm? The cable's stopped!

                    TRUDY
It didn't stop. It was cut.

                    MAURA
Don't say that.

                    TRUDY
I'm just saying what I see.

                    HENRY
(muttering)
Down there. Always down there.

                    DEZ
What's down there? Henry? What's he talking about?

                    MAURA
Leave him be.

                    JOEL
It's coming up.

                    TRUDY
Joel, honey--

                    JOEL
I can hear its feet.

The scraping grows louder. Nearer. The bulb dims.

                    DEZ
(to himself)
Okay okay okay okay.

                    MAURA
Dez, shut the gate. Now.

                    DEZ
It's shut!

                    TRUDY
It's not latched.

                    MAURA
Latch it!

Dez fumbles at the bolt. His hands shake. Below, the scrape becomes a heavy wet drag, and the cage trembles on its cable.

                    HENRY
(whispering)
He said we'd never dig this deep.

The bulb goes out.

Darkness. Breathing. Joel starts to whimper.

Something knocks, once, gently, on the underside of the floor.
